=== f34a1c345f
shader: negate the constant in FFMA's register-register-constant form

In the native FFMA form with the constant buffer as the third operand
(0x623), bit 75 negates that constant, like bit 63. The lowering applied
it to the register in bits 64-71 instead. Splatoon Raiders linearizes
its depth buffer with FFMA R0, R0, R7, -c[0x3][0x0] (R7 holds the same
constant): the result came out negated, every shadow lookup landed
outside the shadow map and the island had no shadows. Its shadow pass
negates the same kind of constant with bit 63, which was already right.

The disk shader cache keeps the lowered code, so the Vulkan and OpenGL
cache versions go up to drop programs lowered the old way.

Ported-from: https://github.com/Djihads80/Eglantine 2abe49ad7697f60ae29595984a1c6df0a53d9739

=== c95e43c7b8
vulkan: keep the 69-byte vertex repack off on NVIDIA

NVIDIA's driver fetches the unaligned 69-byte vertex layout correctly:
Splatoon Raiders rendered right on an RTX 4070 before the repack. There
the repack only adds a compute pass per draw, and in two RTX 4070
sessions some of those meshes (the tutorial island's palm tree and
cliffs) came out as stretched streaks with it on, never with it off.

The repack stays on by default for the other drivers.
EGLANTINE_TEST_VERTEX_REPACK=1 turns it on everywhere, =0 off everywhere.

Ported-from: https://github.com/Djihads80/Eglantine 2fee8589afbc43d103d19320103966acaa80a786

=== 142e161fea
buffer_cache: cache read areas for CPU bytes on GPU-written pages

The first profile run of the read back split showed about 97M guest reads
taking both cache locks, 94M of them reading no GPU data. Their read areas
covered only the read itself, so each new address asked the GPU.

The area now covers the GPU-free bytes around the read within its page. A
GPU write onto a handed out area drops every core's read areas.

RangeSet::ForEachInRange now ignores empty queries: at address 0, as in a
null binding, one visited every range with end < begin.

Builds only: lock rate, the stutter in issue 180 and colliders not retested.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (analysis and implementation)

Ported-from: https://github.com/Djihads80/Eglantine 638795281827e5dd8fb7c3fb09551567f3b875d7

=== 9666c57900
vk_staging_buffer_pool: free idle staging buffers, round large ones in quarter steps

Staging buffers were checked for release one size class per frame, 16 at
a time, and any buffer the GPU was done with was freed, even one requests
took every frame, so it was created again right after. Every class is now
checked each frame, and a buffer no request took for 60 frames is freed
once the GPU is done with it, at most 32 per frame, largest first.

Sizes above 1 MiB rounded up to the next power of two, so a 17 MiB
upload took a 32 MiB buffer. They now round up to a quarter step (1.25,
1.5, 1.75 or 2 times the power of two below), and a request may reuse a
free buffer up to two classes larger.

Mario Kart World, title screen for 6 minutes (one run each, RTX 4070
Laptop): staging memory at boot peaks at 1,175 MiB instead of 1,478 MiB,
4,510 staging buffers are created instead of 13,168, and Windows' shared
GPU memory averages 417 MiB instead of 676 MiB.

Ported-from: https://github.com/Djihads80/Eglantine f5e4384effdf8c747253991cbd4e6f9c68ccafc1

=== 7b684c444c
buffer_cache: download only the pages the guest reads back

A page now joins the read back set only when a guest read overlaps
GPU-written bytes, and each GPU write splits into the part on those pages
(downloaded at fences) and the rest (read traps). Previously any slow-path
read marked its page, and one marked page made every write of the whole
binding download at fences, for good.

Reads of CPU bytes keep the page's trap armed so its GPU bytes still flush.
Adds EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE counters for downloads and guest reads.

Builds only: the stutter in issue 180 and the colliders are not retested.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (analysis and implementation)

Ported-from: https://github.com/Djihads80/Eglantine c75fa8b3f9995682cf56c01969cc9e4c09fe0bbd

