=== 3ef8031da6
tools/nx2: shader catalogue for the shader lab

The shader lab can now name programs and keep those names. Each game gets
tools/nx2/catalog/<program id>.json: what a program draws, its stage and how
it was found, keyed by native program key (the same in every build) with the
shader hash next to it.

The lab merges the repo copy with a local copy that carries over between
builds (EDEN_NX2_CATALOG_DIR, default %APPDATA%/eglantine/nx2-catalog or
~/.local/share/eglantine/nx2-catalog) and saves edits there. It shows the
names in the program list, has a catalogue view, and fills in the key of an
entry known only by its hash when it sees that program. tools/nx2/catalog.py
compares, merges and adds entries from the command line.

AGENTS.md asks agents to run catalog.py status before each commit, and to
commit the merged catalogue when theirs has more than the repo's.

It starts with 43 Ocarina of Time programs from the Kokiri Forest work, 19 of
them with keys so far. Also fixes the lab's --parent-pid check on Windows,
where os.kill(pid, 0) sends CTRL_C_EVENT instead of checking the process.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (implementation, testing, commit message draft)
Signed-off-by: Duck912 <350528965+Duck912@users.noreply.github.com>

=== 7602efc518
debugger, video_core: port Lichii's live debug server and frame dump

Brings Zortos' debug tools from Lichii (zortos293/Lichii #56, #111 and the
debug parts of #184) over, so a dev or an agent can ask a running game
questions without relaunching it.

EDEN_NX2_DEBUG_SERVER=127.0.0.1:<port> starts a loopback server once a game
has loaded: status, screenshot, log tail and filter, capture_frame (the frame
dump, optionally with sampled textures and bound buffers), reject_shaders
(EDEN_NX2_REJECT_HASH at runtime), guest memory read and search, GPU memory
read, input and input_sequence, and the sampler. tools/nx2/debug_mcp.py makes
it an MCP server, or runs one request from a shell with --call. write_memory
is only available with EDEN_NX2_DEBUG_WRITE=1. docs/nx2/debug-mcp.md has the
details.

The frame dump also works on its own (EDEN_NX2_FRAME_DUMP*). Captures and
rejects take native program keys as well as shader hashes, and the shader
lab's diag-state.json now has the game's title and each program's shader
hash.

Everything is off unless its variable is set. The sampler moved from
yuzu_cmd to common so the server can use it; eden-cli's EDEN_NX2_SAMPLE works
as before.

Tested with Ocarina of Time in eden-cli on Windows (RTX 5080): status,
screenshot, log tail, a live reject and restore, both capture types, memory
search and read, and stick input all worked.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (porting, implementation, testing, commit message draft)
Signed-off-by: Duck912 <350528965+Duck912@users.noreply.github.com>

=== 1f52ac7e2e
nx2: drop late movie frames so the picture keeps up with the sound

Ocarina of Time shows one decoded frame per frame it draws and never
skips one, while the movie's audio plays in real time. Below 60 fps the
picture falls further behind the sound with every frame: in a user's
log the opening cutscene (7040 frames, 117 s) took 148 s at 48 fps and
ended about 30 s behind its audio. The title video falls behind the same
way (Djihads80/Eglantine#629).

The video session now keeps a movie clock, started from the frames the
game acquires. When an output image comes free, decoded frames that a
later frame has already overtaken on that clock are dropped without
being converted or written, so the next image holds the frame the sound
is at. Frames are only dropped, never held back, and never the
end-of-stream frame. A gap of over 1 s between acquires (a pause) or a
lag over 3 s restarts the clock instead, as do flush, stop, finalize and
a new configure. Streams without rising timestamps are left alone.

At full speed nothing is dropped. EDEN_NX2_VDEC_PACE=0 turns it off.
The decoder log line now counts the dropped frames.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (debugging, implementation, commit message draft)
Signed-off-by: Duck912 <350528965+Duck912@users.noreply.github.com>

=== a23be213d5
nx2: wait for each of Ocarina of Time's frames

Ocarina of Time presents while one of its GPU channels waits, so the
present returned after the previous frame's fence and the game started
the next frame while the GPU thread still read this frame's data from
guest memory. Some frames rendered with the next frame's lighting and
exposure values: one-frame white or overexposed flashes, easiest to see
standing still at the Kokiri spawn.

Wait for the frame's own fence for Ocarina too, as for Donkey Kong
Bananza. This costs some speed (about 5 ms a frame in earlier runs).
EDEN_NX2_FRAMES_AHEAD still overrides it.

Tested on an RTX 5080 with GPU accuracy Fast: EDEN_NX2_FRAMES_AHEAD=0
removed the flashes, which stayed with the compute barriers, Lichii #215
(query results before macro DMA reads) and reactive flushing off.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (debugging, implementation, commit message draft)
Signed-off-by: Duck912 <350528965+Duck912@users.noreply.github.com>

=== 92f119195f
video_core: replace Ocarina's movie network by an input copy

The title menu runs Ocarina's 11-program movie network every frame. Its matrix
multiplies are emulated (the compact form runs, it is not rejected), which costs
seconds per frame on Pascal-class GPUs: the menu sat at 12-19 FPS while the GPU
was not the limit.

A pass that reads one colour image and stores none now retains that colour and
opens a bounded skip window (32 dispatches); the pass that stores an image
upscaling the retained colour is replaced by a plain copy (sRGB decode kept when
the program does it itself) and closes the window. The window is anchored on the
movie network's own input slot (+0x58): opening it on any one-read pass made the
world black, because gameplay compute got skipped too.

The gameplay network is deliberately left alone: its read->store pair
(+0xc0 -> +0xd0) carries the tonemap/lighting composite, and copying it leaves
the frame flat, far too bright and fogged. EDEN_NX2_UPSCALE_REPLACE=0 disables
the replacement.

Measured on a GTX 1060: menu 12-19 -> 60 FPS (16.7 ms), the movie plays, the
world keeps the emulated network and its correct visuals. No other pass or the
Fire Emblem / Fortune's Weave chain machinery is touched.

