=== 292a68df46
video_core: keep 4096 MaxContinuousRange answers per thread

Ocarina of Time binds storage buffers behind loaded pointers on most draws and
dispatches (the vegetation change in 4ac9e703fe), and each bind asks
MaxContinuousRange for up to 64 MiB. That is ~220,000 calls a second at the
Kokiri Forest spawn. The 64-entry cache from 47b4169b3f holds fewer buffers
than the game binds, so ~25,000 calls a second still missed and walked ~7 MiB
of page table each: 44% of the GPU back thread in stack samples, which is what
limits the game's frame rate there.

The cache now keeps 4096 entries per thread (allocated on first use, ~160 KiB
on the threads that bind buffers). Misses drop to ~7,000 a second over much
shorter ranges (walking time ~200 -> ~20 ms per second). The page table never
changed during play (0 table ops a second), so the invalidation stays as it is.

Measured on an MSVC build, CLI harness, Kokiri Forest spawn, GPU accuracy Fast,
600-present averages: 26.3-27.4 ms per frame before (4 runs), 22.3-23.2 ms
after (4 runs), about +20% fps. A 65,536-entry cache was no faster.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (profiling, implementation, drafting)
Signed-off-by: Duck912 <350528965+Duck912@users.noreply.github.com>

=== 743d5bbaed
shader: mesh programs that output points or lines

SET_MESH_SHADER_A's output topology was only implemented for triangles (4);
Ocarina of Time's garden hedges draw mesh programs with topology 0
(SET_MESH_SHADER_A 0x00040200, 32 points per group), whose pipelines failed
with "Mesh output topology 0 is not implemented" and whose draws were
skipped every frame.

Points (0) and lines (1) now use OutputPoints / OutputLinesEXT with
PrimitivePointIndicesEXT / PrimitiveLineIndicesEXT. The map region holds
one index byte per primitive vertex, as for triangles. Point programs write
the fixed state point size to every vertex before the program's own lines,
as Vulkan needs a size per vertex. Triangle programs compile as before.

The pipelines now build. The hedge leaves stay missing: their draws get a
zero count from the vegetation culling compute programs, which are still
rejected.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== 7d26d3b2fb
texture_cache: skip the upload of a new render target a clear overwrites whole (#141)

* texture_cache: skip the upload of a new render target a clear overwrites whole

JoinImages uploads every new image from guest memory before the clear that binds it can invalidate it. Ocarina of Time creates about 4 such targets a frame outdoors, and the uploads were about 15% of the GPU back thread. When UpdateRenderTargets runs for a clear and the clear covers the new single-level, single-layer 2D target whole (all RGBA channels on clear_surface.RT, or Z on zeta, S with a full stencil mask; scissor off or covering it; every bound target at least its size; no join copies), mark it clean and transition it instead of uploading.

Ocarina of Time outdoors (Kokiri Forest): 46.5 and 46.2 fps vs 40.2-42.5 before; burst capture shows no flashes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* texture_cache: let IsFullClear decide before a new clear target skips its upload

JoinImages no longer marks the new target clean. It stays CpuModified, so UpdateRenderTargets' PrepareImage drops the upload only when IsFullClear agrees that the clear covers the whole image, and uploads it otherwise. A partial clear can no longer leave part of the target with uninitialised contents.

