Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
=== 5e2cdc9f1a
common: retry early UMWAIT wakes until the Event deadline (#137)

#120 fixed the MWAITX path of Event::WaitFor and added the Event tests.
Intel CPUs with WAITPKG (Alder Lake and later) take the UMWAIT path
instead, and 7 of the 11 Event tests fail there (i9-12900K).

UMWAIT's result (CF) is set only when the OS limit in IA32_UMWAIT_CONTROL
ended the wait. Every other wake (a store to the monitored line, an
interrupt, the deadline) returned "timed out" without looking at the
event. A Set during the wait was reported as a timeout and left the
event set, and an interrupt ended the wait early.

Retry until the steady_clock deadline the way the MWAITX path does, with
the same bounded cycle budget so the absolute TSC deadline can't wrap.
The fallback for CPUs with neither instruction returned true even when it
timed out. It now waits for the same deadline and returns whether it
consumed the event.

Tested on an i9-12900K (WAITPKG, no MONITORX), Windows 11:
- tests.exe "[event]": 7 of 11 failed before, all pass after (three
  runs). With the WAITPKG path forced off by a temporary switch (not in
  this commit), the fallback passes them too.
- tests.exe: 665 of 668, the 3 known failures.
- Ocarina of Time outside Link's house, old vs new wait on one binary,
  four interleaved rounds each: 0 flashes in every run, 48.8 vs 46.8 fps
  on average, within run-to-run noise (31 to 60 fps).

Not tested: the MWAITX path (AMD) only moved its deadline out to share
it. Windows on ARM has its own WaitFor with the same always-true
fallback, untouched here.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
=== 687a4943e0
common: retry unsignalled MWAITX wakes until the Event deadline (#120)

* common: retry unsignalled MWAITX wakes until the Event deadline

Keep the existing monitor-based waiting policy, but check a fixed saturating deadline after every wake. Bound and round cycle budgets before narrowing to the 32-bit MWAITX counter. Preserve pending-signal consumption for nonpositive durations.

Retain the six Event regressions from Eglantine #538 (a51de161ed) without importing its high-resolution timer policy. Add arithmetic, reset-wake and extreme-duration coverage plus a Windows QPC verification probe. The extreme-duration test also exposes condition_variable wait_for overflow on Linux; use the same saturating deadline with wait_until there.

Co-authored-by: capy-ai[bot] <230910855+capy-ai[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>

* common: make steady clock authoritative for MWAITX timeout expiry

The hardware clock uses a sampled TSC frequency rounded to 100 kHz; its nanoseconds can advance faster than Windows QPC. The prior deadline could therefore expire early on steady_clock even though the MWAITX tick budget rounded upward. Use steady_clock for both deadline construction and expiry, retaining the TSC estimate only for bounded hardware wait durations.

Keep the existing concurrent timeout regression unchanged and add repeated 100 ms and 500 ms steady-clock checks.

Co-authored-by: capy-ai[bot] <230910855+capy-ai[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>

