=== a539f224b9
shader: count nested returns before moving demotes to the end (AMD black scene) (#127)

DemoteCombinationPass, which only runs on AMD drivers (needs_demote_reorder),
moves the program's demotes to just before the last epilogue and is meant to
give up when there is more than one return. It only counted Epilogue blocks at
branch depth 1 or less, so a nested early return was missed.

Native fragment programs end their discarded invocations like that:
'@P1 KILL' followed by '@P0 EXIT P1' becomes if (P0) { if (P1) return; }.
After the reorder those invocations reach the early return before the moved
demote, are never demoted, and write their never-set colour (zero). In
Ocarina of Time (0400486024DEC000) the half-resolution particle composite
(PS e40561a7a0f7a0b3, blend dst * SrcAlpha) then multiplies the whole scene
by zero: on AMD the 3D world is black except around Navi and particles,
while NVIDIA (no reorder) renders it.

Count returns at any depth so such programs keep their demote in place.

Co-authored-by: Inc0rporate <263078185+Inc0rporate@users.noreply.github.com>
Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
=== 3a7477111f
vulkan: skip tessellation pipelines the driver would refuse

A tessellation pipeline with only one of the control/evaluation stages, a
control shader output vertex count outside 1..maxTessellationPatchSize, or on
a device without tessellationShader is skipped like a missing mesh pipeline
(the draw is dropped) instead of failing VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED.
patchControlPoints is clamped to 1..32 and the device limit. A failed build
now logs its stages, patch size and the device maximum.

=== 8d356dfa56
vulkan: say why a draw was skipped, and cap async-build skips separately

The 'Draw skipped (no pipeline)' line gave no reason, and with asynchronous shaders on its 64-line budget went to expected async-build skips, hiding real failures. It now ends in miss=<reason>, and async-build lines have their own cap of 8.

=== 23457ccf7b
nx2: keep other threads out of the Vulkan scheduler while the split's back submits

Two paths from #494 let a second thread use the scheduler at the same time as
the GPU back thread, which crashed in Scheduler::SubmitExecution (SIGSEGV,
SEGV_MAPERR, from GpuSplit::BackLoop -> Scheduler::FlushDeferred) in Mario
Kart World and other games.

- The back runs its idle hook (the deferred fence / list-copy flush) after it
  has published everything as consumed, so GpuSplit::Drain() could return and
  the front run work of its own (MapInOrder) while that submit was going on.
  The back now clears back_quiet before it executes entries and sets it after
  the idle hook; Drain() waits for it as well and asks the back to run the
  hook at once when it is still spinning.
- BufferCache::WriteMemory downloads the GPU-written bytes on written pages
  (b13c4c1c0c), which records and submits work. Device memory unmaps and
  writes, ThreadManager::InvalidateRegion and FlushAndInvalidateRegion reach it
  on CPU and service threads. It now downloads only on the thread that owns
  the scheduler (GpuSplit::OwnsScheduler: the back while it runs, else the GPU
  thread). Guest CPU writes still go through TryCacheInvalidation, which sends
  such pages to the GPU thread.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (diagnosis from tester stack traces, fix)

=== e88024e6b0
shader, video_core: run Ocarina's end-of-frame network through a compact software HMMA

Ocarina of Time draws its final image through an 11-program network after the
tonemap. Its HMMA programs were rejected ("native matrix multiply") and the
stand-in blitted the network's input to its output, which leaves the frame
flat and far too bright: Kokiri Forest median luma ~0.29 at the spawn spot
against ~0.10 on a build that runs the network, and 0.05-0.08 in native
footage. EDEN_NX2_TENSOR=1 runs it through the inline expansion, but the
programs grow so large that the first load takes minutes to compile.

HMMA can now lower to TENSOR_MUL_ADD, a Maxwell-encoded pseudo-instruction
(bit 48 marks it, bit 49 selects K = 8) that the translator turns into an IR
TensorMulAdd. The SPIR-V backend defines one loop helper per K depth and
program and calls it at each site: the same lane mapping, f32 sums and half
rounding after every eight products as the inline expansion, with subgroup
shuffles. GLSL and GLASM don't implement it.

It is used for Ocarina of Time only (KeplerCompute::UsesCompactTensorPrograms)
and only where compute shaders are created 32 threads wide; a guarded HMMA
keeps the inline expansion. EDEN_NX2_TENSOR=compact forces it for other
titles. Fire Emblem's DLSS network and Donkey Kong Bananza keep the inline
expansion. Pipeline cache versions bumped (Vulkan 30, OpenGL 27).

Ported from Lichii (zortos293/Lichii #5 by Mattscreative, current form from 8cd5c6a44b),
which uses the compact form for every title; its NV cooperative-matrix path
and EDEN_NX2_NO_TENSOR switch are not taken. The SPIR-V tests come from
Lichii's spirv_software_matrix.cpp; the lowering test is new.

Co-authored-by: Mattscreative <84041391+ryzendew@users.noreply.github.com>
Co-authored-by: Zortos <65777760+zortos293@users.noreply.github.com>
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (port, per-title gate, tests)

=== 993a31c593
shader: gather each tap with its own offset when per-tap offsets stay dynamic

A TLD4.PTP whose offsets the IR can't fold to constants was emitted as a
plain gather: "Not all arguments in PTP are immediate, ignoring" (eight
programs per Ocarina of Time boot, logged on Linux and Windows), so all four
taps read the footprint at the coordinate instead of at their own offsets.
SPIR-V's ConstOffsets has to be constant.

Such gathers now issue one gather per tap with that tap's runtime Offset and
keep its i0,j0 texel (w), which is the texel a per-tap gather returns for
that tap. Gathers with constant offsets, and sparse gathers, are unchanged.
Pipeline cache versions bumped (Vulkan 29, OpenGL 26).

Ported from Duck912 1e02498099 (oot-build18).

