=== 575d0a5bfd
buffer_cache: upload CPU writes onto GPU-written pages instead of downloading them

Ocarina performance patch:

WriteMemory keeps GPU-written bytes that share a page with a CPU write by
downloading the page first (the Hyrule Warriors fix). Each of those downloads
finishes the scheduler and waits for the host GPU. Ocarina writes small
structures next to GPU data about 230 times a second, which kept the split's
back thread waiting 60% of the time.

For Switch 2 titles, write the CPU's bytes into the buffer through
InlineMemoryImplementation instead, as CachedWriteMemory already does: the
page stays GPU-owned, the GPU bytes next to the write are kept, and nothing
waits. Ranges no buffer covers still take the download path.
  EDEN_NX2_INLINE_PAGE_WRITES=0/1 forces the choice.

Assisted by: Claude Code/Opus 5.5

(cherry picked from commit c8ea816e8ae536f39c1a70796fe0fbc0e3fb7d5e)

=== 5d742e369e
buffer_cache: download only the Preflushable parts of a GPU write at fences

A GPU write that touched any Preflushable page was queued whole, so large
storage buffers with one CPU-read page were copied back at every fence
(about 5 GB/s in Ocarina of Time's forest). Queue the marked pages and trap
reads of the rest.

A CPU read is only served by the fence downloads when all GPU-written data
in its range is marked; otherwise it still flushes. The core keeps the read
area either way, so the area's trapped ranges are cleared and the next GPU
write to it arms them again.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (profiling, fix and flicker debugging)
(cherry picked from commit e7ec986a511bb8383e4bdd398b857f9c97b90d5a)

=== 8acfc248b3
video_core: invalidate caches after MemoryManager::WriteBlock copies the data

WriteBlockImpl invalidated a region before copying the new bytes into it.
The buffer cache's inline page-write path uploads the written range from
guest memory and leaves it GPU-owned, so it uploaded the old bytes and the
new ones were never marked for upload. Invalidate after the copy, as
DeviceMemoryManager::WriteBlock does. The download path is unaffected:
WriteMemory removes the written range from the GPU-modified ranges before
downloading, so the new bytes aren't overwritten.

Assisted by: Claude Code/Opus 5.5

(cherry picked from commit acf3887bbb742169fe4bac7eba17accdd9c3ce58)

=== 47b4169b3f
video_core: cache MaxContinuousRange until the GPU page table changes

Native storage buffers size each binding by walking up to 8 MiB of the
page table on every bind. In Ocarina of Time's forest that ran about
70,000 times a second and took 17% of the GPU thread. Keep the answers
per thread until a map or unmap changes the table.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (profiling and fix)
(cherry picked from commit 5f8cd77fe814b9f184f506e132d6e4f832f4acb1)

