=== 565253831a
vulkan: split large vertex repacks instead of refusing them

A binding whose range was over one dispatch (about 32 MB at 65535
groups) or over the storage buffer range (128 MB on some mobile GPUs)
was not repacked, but the vertex input still used the 72-byte stride
and moved offsets. BindVertexBuffers then bound the 69-byte buffer under
the 72-byte layout, and BindVertexBuffer bound nothing, leaving the
previous draw's buffer in place.

The repack now runs as several dispatches, each within the limits and
with its output at an aligned offset. A binding that still is not
repacked (a null buffer, or limits too small for one aligned step)
drops its bit for the draw, so the vertex input built after the buffers
are bound keeps the 69-byte layout.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (implementation and tests)

=== 212ac74d11
vulkan: repack 69-byte vertices to 72 bytes (#281)

A game draws with a 69-byte vertex stride and a packed 4-byte
attribute at byte 65. Those fetches are unaligned, which Vulkan leaves
undefined: NVIDIA reads them as meant, the AMD Windows driver does not,
and the outdoor scenes turn into a white haze with horizontal streaks.

When a binding uses that layout, a compute pass copies it to a 72-byte
stride with the tail moved to byte 68, and the vertex input offsets and
stride follow. EGLANTINE_TEST_VERTEX_REPACK=0 turns it off;
EGLANTINE_EXPERIMENTAL_STATIC_VERTEX_REPACK=1 also covers pipelines
without dynamic vertex input.

Based on an outside patch (02-portable-vertex-repacking). Changes from
it: a range over the device limits skips the repack instead of throwing,
and the pipeline cache version stays, so caches are kept.

(cherry picked from commit 974bc548fc00bc130496af17cd584cf6a3796131)

