=== e19170b7af
vulkan: share one zeroed block between empty sparse blocks on AMD

AMD's drivers can't sample images with unbound memory, so large sparse
images are backed whole there, a game's nine sparse masks
included (about 2.9 GB, re-uploaded whole for every tile the game maps).

Where the driver supports sparseResidencyAliased, SparseTiles images are
now created with VK_IMAGE_CREATE_SPARSE_ALIASED_BIT and every block is
bound to one zeroed 64 KiB block when the image is created (the image is
cleared once); a block gets memory of its own when data arrives, as on
other drivers. Nothing is ever left unbound. If the GPU renders into such
an image, every block gets memory of its own first, cleared, and the data
is loaded again, so the shared block stays zero.
EDEN_NX2_SPARSE_ALIASED=1 uses the shared block wherever it is supported,
=0 never.

Radeon 780M (proprietary driver 32.0.31041), a game title
screen, 200 s: no hang or device loss, world lit, shared memory 12.7 GB
at the end growing 1.8 GB/min, against 18.7 GB and 5.1 GB/min with the
masks backed whole. With every sparse image unbound instead (no AMD
fallback at all) the static world renders dark there, so the other large
sparse images stay backed whole on AMD.

(cherry picked from commit 130c255ed91e2bf8f726b38101cc5fc2abcf3935)

=== b8601bef8b
texture_cache: back large sparse 2D images by sparse block

A game's nine world-sized masks (R8 16384x16384, RG16 and
R16 8192x8192, 170-341 MB each) live in sparse regions the game maps
its own memory into, a few MiB of each. Since a mapped tile counts as
written, the texture cache backed and uploaded each mask in full: about
2.9 GB of device memory, and a whole-mask upload for every tile the
game streamed in.

Such images (single layer, one texel per block, colour, in a sparse
region, 32 MiB or more) are now unbacked SparseTiles images. The
texture cache records the guest ranges written to them; Image::BindTiles
gives the host sparse blocks under those ranges memory, and only those
rectangles are unswizzled, a row of whole GOB blocks at a time, and
uploaded. A new image starts with the tiles the game maps there (nx2:gpu
216 now records them per 64 KiB), or the written blocks where it maps
none. Unmapped tiles keep their blocks and are loaded as zeros.

Checked against the full unswizzle on 400 ordinary game images (row
padding, mip chains, odd sizes): no row differs. Not on AMD drivers,
which cannot sample images with unbound memory.

A game title screen, 150 s, against backing the masks whole:
dedicated VRAM 6.6 -> 6.2 GB, shared 1.0 -> 0.3 GB, peak 8.2 -> 6.9 GB.

(cherry picked from commit 1c794af56407f324eedad99478388ba0c9cf9ad6)

=== b370a21460
vulkan: back large sparse images normally on AMD

Lazy Switch 2 sparse regions made the texture cache create large images
with no memory bound and sample them. AMD's drivers crash shortly after
