=== b98190e8fc
nx2: count a mapped sparse tile as written

With remap support a game fills nine world-sized masks
(R8 16384x16384, RG16 and R16 8192x8192, one sparse region each) in its
own memory and then maps them in through 216. Nothing writes them through
the sparse region, so its blocks stayed unwritten and the texture cache
kept the images over them without memory, reading zero: the static world
(roads, buildings, rocks, trees) rendered unlit while karts and cars were
lit. Without remap support the game never maps them and lights the world
without them.

A tile map now counts its blocks as written and invalidates the caches
over the tile, so those images get memory and load what the game mapped.
That costs the masks' full size in device memory (about 2.9 GB in a game), which is what they need until the texture cache can back
single images tile by tile. DeviceMemoryManager::Unmap no longer needs
its flag to keep the caches.

(cherry picked from commit 139b7e5efb7c4181f14e987706eef26ca1f327c0)

=== 838fc14705
nx2: map and unmap sparse texture tiles (nx2:gpu 216)

Switch 2 games commit and release the memory behind sparse textures by
remapping tiles: nvnMemoryPoolMapVirtual goes through NvRm's remap into
nn::gpu::sdk::ExecuteSparseMapRequests, nx2:gpu sub-session command 216,
a batch of 40-byte requests. A map points a tile at the game's own
GPU-accessible memory (the handle sub 101 returned, plus an offset), an
unmap makes it read zero again; the sparse region itself never has memory.

NvRm only remaps when the GPU characteristics report
NVGPU_GPU_FLAGS_SUPPORT_REMAP (bit 35). Without it every remap failed
before reaching the server, so a game streamed its terrain into
unmapped sparse memory and each 4 MiB block it touched got memory that
was never freed (2.5 GB after 10 minutes, still growing).

Report the flag (EDEN_NX2_SPARSE_REMAP=0 leaves it out) and serve 216:

- Sparse regions: a tile's device pages show the game's memory, or are
  unmapped, in GPU thread order, without invalidating the caches over
  them; the region keeps its GPU mapping, so the texture cache keeps its
  images. A region the game maps tiles into gets no more blocks (writes
  outside its tiles are dropped, as on the console), gives back the
  blocks it already had, and marks a block written when the GPU first
  writes it, so array layers it only maps tiles into get no memory or
  uploads.
- GPU-only regions (214 without the sparse flag): the game also sends
  216 for its render targets and, with remap support, for two texture
  arrays (5.8 GB and 1.4 GB). Regions whose memory another GPU-only
  region maps (render-target groups) keep the memory 214 gave them:
  applied, the texture cache flushed and re-uploaded the overlapping
  render targets every frame. Regions 214 backs as they are written
  keep their blocks too, since the texture cache reads an image's memory
  in full when it uploads it, which makes the game's pages resident on
  the host (about 1 GB more 150 s in). The rest get the game's memory
  the way 211 maps it, so writes through the game's own address reach
  the same device pages; an unmap points the range back at the region's
  own memory.

A game, 10 minutes: no sparse blocks backed (2.5 GB before),
24.6 presents a second instead of 19.3. 216 totals are logged every 30 s.

(cherry picked from commit 4d6a13e3d9eaa7bf17381fce0e50d5275ac8957e)

=== 5f2e0f0f81
vulkan_device: keep an integrated GPU's budget within its memory share

CollectPhysicalMemoryInfo gives an integrated GPU 4 GiB of device memory
(6 GiB with aggressive VRAM use), since its device-local heap is system
memory. GetDeviceMemoryBudget returned the heap's driver budget instead
(21178 MiB on a Radeon 780M), and the texture cache follows that budget
for Switch 2 titles: it collected only past 20.6 GB, filling system RAM,
and kept ASTC uncompressed (the BC3 switch looks at the budget too). Cap
it at that share.

Radeon 780M, a game title screen, 200 s: shared GPU memory
2.7 -> 3.6 GB instead of 7.6 -> 12.7 GB (0.25 instead of 1.8 GB/min),
private bytes 12.9 instead of 22.4 GB at the end, ASTC re-compressed to
BC3, same frame rate.

(cherry picked from commit 39b9892f000b3ae943d3dd72a8729eaf71db0595)

