(cherry picked from commit eabe07d3158587e22ea4cbfc8e25ea4ca73a0403)

=== 452dc27367
vk_graphics_pipeline: share shader modules between pipelines

GraphicsPipeline now holds its SPIR-V modules through a
shared_ptr<const GraphicsShaderModules>, so pipelines whose keys differ
only in state the shaders do not see can use the same modules. The
modules live until the last pipeline using them is destroyed. No
behaviour change: every pipeline still builds its own modules.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit 0bc4d465702d863bd9d37e4aadb05bf8f2a10127)

=== 9a876a744e
vk_pipeline_cache: translate native graphics pipelines on worker threads

A new graphics pipeline was translated (Maxwell code -> IR -> SPIR-V) on the GPU thread before
its draw, ~2-4 ms each, which is most of the first-lap stutter left in title 1 with
asynchronous shaders on. For native programs whose draw may be skipped (same rule as for a
pipeline whose driver compile is not done), the GPU thread now builds and detaches the
environments, queues translation and driver compile on new translation workers (half the
pipeline worker count, so compute compiles are not held up) and waits up to a budget
(EDEN_NX2_ASYNC_TRANSLATION_WAIT_MS, default 3 ms, 0 = never) on the job's future. Ready in
time: the draw uses it. Otherwise the draw is skipped and the pipeline adopted into
graphics_cache by a later draw; the disk cache and driver cache flush are queued at adoption,
on the GPU thread, as for pipelines built there. Compute pipelines and Switch 1 programs are
unchanged. EDEN_NX2_ASYNC_TRANSLATION=0 turns the new path off; a log line every 10 s counts
queued, ready-in-budget and adopted pipelines and their GPU-thread and worker time.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit 99bbe1f5ecce86bbce02e4e4484f8c68174a474c)

=== 6d8f95f08e
shader_environment: let graphics environments detach from the 3D engine

Translation reads live 3D engine state through the environment (constant buffer values,
texture descriptors through the texture pool registers, the viewport transform flag, HLE
replacements), which is why it had to run on the GPU thread. Detach() copies that state:
the stage's enabled constant buffers whole (their values get folded into the shader and
uniform memory is reused once the GPU is past the draw), the texture pool's device address
(read later with unsynchronized reads, through the device memory manager, which outlives the
GPU address space at shutdown), the viewport flag and the replacement table. It then drops
the engine pointer. It refuses, leaving the environment as it was, when the pool is not one
contiguous device range. ShaderCache::device_memory becomes protected for the pipeline cache.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit adf315b3fc3ea34f602ff226e3d7bda8f9209b33)

=== 2b1eda0eb1
shader_recompiler: create SSA phis of sealed blocks only when they are needed

When a read reaches a sealed block with several predecessors and no
definition, the pass created an operandless phi first, to break cycles,
read every predecessor, then removed the phi again when it was trivial,
which it almost always is (222,000 of 226,000 phis in the largest Mario
Kart World fragment shader). Each one was allocated, linked in, filled,
unlinked, reinserted as an Identity and later dropped.

The phi now stays pending: the block is marked, the operands are kept on
the side, and the phi is only created when a cycle reads it or when it
merges two different values. Nothing is inserted at the front of the
block in between, so it is created at the same place with the same
operands; trivial pending phis leave nothing behind, as the removed
phis' unused identities did not either.

Emitted SPIR-V is identical for the 11,883 modules of two Mario Kart
World disk caches (eden-shader-bench --hashes).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit 9c0383a9c086b7832fa3510fcbea1c207b883e27)

=== 692780fab9
shader_recompiler: find the visited block containing a label by lookup

CFG::InspectVisitedBlocks scanned every block of the function for each
label it analyzed, which made CFG construction quadratic in the number of
blocks. The blocks are kept sorted by their first instruction and never
overlap (virtual blocks are empty), so the only candidate is the last
non-empty block starting at or before the label: find it from
upper_bound. CFG time in the benchmark: 337 ms -> 181 ms.

Emitted SPIR-V is identical for the 11,883 modules of two Mario Kart
World disk caches (eden-shader-bench --hashes).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit 11d26dde666f6c73d8243cd1a7dcf6f9d7a3a3f3)

=== 8b32fcb629
spirv: remember the ids of scalar constants

Every immediate operand goes through EmitContext::Const, and Sirit's
Constant deduplicates by building a std::vector of the instruction words
and hashing it: ~10% of the benchmark's profile, half of it in the
allocator. EmitContext now keeps the id of each u32 and f32 constant it
asked Sirit for, keyed by bit pattern as Sirit does, and returns it for
later uses. Sirit would have returned the same id, so the module does
not change.

Emitted SPIR-V is identical for the 11,883 modules of two Mario Kart
World disk caches (eden-shader-bench --hashes).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit 87734a7800a180f934a2acc9444b262d6b6ccb13)

=== 981247484f
shader_recompiler: keep SSA definitions of predicates and flags in blocks

Registers already store their SSA definitions in the blocks. Predicates,
the condition flags and the indirect branch variable used one node-based
std::unordered_map each, keyed by block, and goto variables a map of such
maps; every read walks back through blocks, looking up (and inserting) a
definition in each. Those lookups and their allocations were ~13% of a
profile of the benchmark.

The fixed-count variables now live in the blocks next to the registers,
goto variables in one flat map keyed by (block, index). Lookups no longer
insert empty entries, which only the lookups themselves could see.

Emitted SPIR-V is identical for the 11,883 modules of two Mario Kart
World disk caches (eden-shader-bench --hashes).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit 1fcc877fe71a7e7c448aca78ed35662ff7157e75)

=== ce96865006
shader_recompiler: skip instructions failed storage buffer searches already walked

GlobalMemoryToStorageBufferPass memoizes searches by start instruction,
but in the 28,000-instruction compute shader of NX2_NOTES row 121 every
one of its 725 global accesses starts from a different address and every
biased search fails after walking ~5,600 instructions: 560 ms.

BreadthFirstSearchPruned keeps, per predicate, the instructions a failed
search visited (nothing reachable from them matches) and skips them in
later searches. Instructions not reachable from skipped ones are only
queued by instructions that are not either, so they are visited in the
same order and the result is the one BreadthFirstSearch returns. That
shader now translates in 75 ms instead of 593 ms.

Emitted SPIR-V is identical for the 11,883 modules of two Mario Kart
World disk caches (eden-shader-bench --hashes).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit b66f74d83262a3ac003159c9d28122ac2f4d5e61)

=== a3c4ce20a1
shader_recompiler: drop unused identities at the end of the SSA rewrite

Reads walk back through every block until they find a definition, and
each multi-predecessor block on the way gets an operandless phi that is
usually trivial. Those phis, and the replaced register reads, stay in
their blocks as Identity instructions: a 2,572-block Mario Kart World
fragment shader got 222,000 of them for 126,000 real instructions, and
every pass until dead code elimination walked them. Those without uses
are now removed when the pass ends, in the order dead code elimination
would have removed them.

ReadVariable also reuses one walk stack instead of growing a new vector
on every read.

Emitted SPIR-V is identical for the 11,883 modules of two Mario Kart
World disk caches (eden-shader-bench --hashes).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYitykHUVF1YKAGcYZ8W8G
(cherry picked from commit 55f28f57cf661754a5a08302990ebdb628147588)

=== 800f7feaf6
fix: restore Mario Kart World text by accepting R0 shader returns

Do not treat the reserved destination field of Ampere RET as a write to the return-link register. Keep return linkage validation and rejection of actual link-register writes.

Add three regression tests covering R0 and R55 links, copied links, and overwritten links. Validation: 10 assertions pass; user confirmed the compiled patch restores text in Mario Kart World.

AI-assisted implementation and commit message using OpenAI Codex.

(cherry picked from commit a4a746b47b1d1ee02ddf1fe528c7db05800eec2c)

=== ba11b6d399
nx2_gsv: stop scaling image sizes by preference words 6 and 7

The multiplier (size *= words[6] * words[7], up to 4096x) is a guess at what
the two unconfirmed words mean. Mario Kart World passes 1/1 or 4/1 and then
aborts with 0x3200F3 right after its first draws: the sizes it asks for and
the ones it maps no longer agree.

Bisected on nx2-port: a0fae9746a is the first bad commit; with the multiplier
removed Mario Kart World boots again (both accuracy levels, 3-minute runs).

