=== 9f9fe9f73d
vulkan: bind memory to sparse terrain arrays layer by layer

The written part of Mario Kart World's sparse terrain arrays is small:
13-26% of their blocks. The two 1.45 GB ASTC arrays were backed in full
on the host, and the two 5.8 GB BC6H arrays, too large to back, stayed
unbacked, so what the game streamed into them never showed.

A color array of 32 MiB or more lying in one sparse region, in a format
uploaded layer by layer (huge ASTC arrays decoded on the GPU, or formats
sampled as they are), is now a sparse resident image (SparseLayers).
When it is refreshed, the layers written since (or, for a new image,
the layers any of whose blocks was written) get device memory bound
with vkQueueBindSparse (under the submit lock, waited on, as the
multi-range buffer cache does) and only those layers are uploaded.
Unwritten layers read zero. One image binds at most 768 MiB (384 MiB on
GPUs with less than 7 GiB); rendering into one binds all its layers.

Mario Kart World, 180 s: the ASTC arrays hold 1.4 MiB per written layer,
four BC6H layers (341 MiB) now have their data, device memory in use
4.8-5.0 GB instead of 6.0-7.1 GB with the texture cache below its
collection threshold, 16.8-17.2 ms average frames, peak working set
13.4 GB.

(cherry picked from commit 942c51c0b4575d0eb63430de824ccbee5a45ac86)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== c5128c878b
texture_cache: give images over unwritten sparse blocks no device memory

Mario Kart World creates nine terrain layer pools in sparse regions (four
R8 16384x16384, four RG16 8192x8192, one R16 8192x8192: 2.9 GB of device
memory). In 180 s of title and attract demo not one of their blocks is
written and nothing renders into them, so they hold zeros; on the console
only the few tiles the game maps have memory.

nx2:gpu now publishes which 4 MiB blocks of each sparse region have been
written (nx2.h, a leaf lock the texture cache may take). An image of 32
MiB or more lying in one sparse region with no written block is created
as a sparse resident image with nothing bound: reads return zero, as an
unmapped tile does. The first write into its range, or the first time the
GPU renders into it (that range then counts as written), marks it
remapped, so a backed image replaces it at its next use.

Mario Kart World, 180 s: the collector deletes 0-80 images per 600 frames
instead of thousands, frames over 100 ms per 600 presents drop from 1-9 to
0-1, peak working set 14.7 GB. One render target in a small sparse region
gets replaced once (logged).

(cherry picked from commit a501502d488f54f59d9bb9d2afd284da5318eb72)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== 666a74567d
texture_cache: re-decode only the written layers of large ASTC arrays

Any write into an image marked all of it CPU-modified, so each terrain
tile Mario Kart World streams into its 8192x8192x68 ASTC arrays re-read
and re-decoded the whole 1.45 GB array: a 2 GiB staging buffer and a
full GPU wait each time, 15-16 GB of uploads per 600 frames.

Large layered images on the accelerated ASTC path (64 MiB and up) now
record the layers written since their last upload and stay tracked; a
refresh reads and decodes only those layers, one dispatch per layer and
level into a single-layer storage view. The texture cache's 600-frame
report counts uploads from guest memory, their size and how many went by
layer. Upload scratch buffers grown past 64 MiB for one huge image (341
MiB for the terrain layer pools) are freed at the next frame instead of
kept for the session.

Mario Kart World, 180 s: after loading, uploads drop from 15-16 GB to
0.2-0.5 GB per 600 frames, the largest from 1453 MiB to 2-15 MiB.

(cherry picked from commit 26f4924999c8d603474d5bb3c1d6a0c977fa04ac)

Merged with the partial page reload (7e8c527): layer uploads take accelerated ASTC arrays, partial pages the rest; UploadInChunks kept.

