=== 1e9ac5453a
memory: skip clearing guest memory that is still zero

The kernel clears every page it hands out, and the backing memory is a
page-file section the host only gives RAM to once a page is touched. So
the clears themselves made every page of a game's heap resident: Mario
Kart World (12 GB layout) had a 12.5 GB working set 20 s after boot.

HostMemory keeps two bits per 64 KiB of backing: used (handed out by the
kernel at least once) and dirty (may hold data). Pages returned to the
kernel mark their granules dirty, and a zero clear only touches dirty
granules; a granule cleared in full is clean again. A new kernel on the
same memory marks everything a previous one used dirty, so leaked
processes can't leak data into the next game. KMemoryManager's
optimized-process fill and KSharedMemory's clear go through the same
path.

Mario Kart World, title screen and attract demo, 180 s: working set 20 s
in 4.7-6.4 GB instead of 12.5 GB, peak 14-17.6 GB instead of 20.1 GB.

(cherry picked from commit f260bcb5a26a6b6b9675b41969a273858f024c55)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== cc66e552c2
memory_manager: notify the caches once per changed range

PageTableOp and BigPageTableOp called ModifyGPUMemory for every page
whose entry changed, and each call scans the texture and buffer caches.
Mapping one of Mario Kart World's 1.45 GB sparse regions made ~370,000
of those scans. Runs of changed pages are now reported as one range.

(cherry picked from commit 71f3d8d304204fc49e004041dea0973682ed6fc6)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== 12c2fcc12c
dynarmic: skip untouched locations when an IR instruction ends

Compiling a block is most of what makes Mario Kart World stutter in its
first minute: the game's thread translates ~20,000 blocks in a few
hundred milliseconds when a scene loads, and the frame waits. A CPU
profile of those bursts had ~40% of the compile time in two loops over
all 96 host locations (16 GPRs, 16 XMMs, 64 spill slots) per IR
instruction:

- EndOfAllocScope called ReleaseAll on every location, though an
  instruction touches a few. It now skips the ones ReleaseAll would not
  change (no references or locks, not scratch, no last use pending, and
  values either still live or fully cleared).
- DefineValueImpl checked, with an assert that stays on in release
  builds, that the defined value was in no location yet, and afterwards
  that it was where it was put. Those scans are DEBUG_ASSERTs now.

Mario Kart World, first 45 s (boot, world map, title, attract demo):
18.5 -> 12.8 us per compiled block; frames over 60 ms 57 -> 31, over
100 ms in the first 600 presents 23 -> 12; frames render as before.

(cherry picked from commit e6d53e728a93fe6d97a243de41301b9f7c54df5a)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== a2cc455fa3
dynarmic: keep the x64 JIT's per-block tables compact

Each emulated core has its own JIT, and Mario Kart World runs six. After
three minutes of its title screen and attract demo, their bookkeeping
held ~1.65 GB besides ~470 MB of compiled code, mostly empty slots:

- patch_information: four small_vectors of code pointers per linked
  block (232 bytes a slot), and Patch() added an entry for every block
  compiled, linked or not (~800 MB);
- block_ranges: an interval map with a hash set per segment (~300 bytes
  a block, ~460 MB) to answer invalidations, which only come when code
  is unmapped or reprotected;
- fastmem_patch_info: 48 bytes per fastmem access in a hash map
  (~340 MB).

Patch locations are now offsets into the code space with the patch kind
in the low two bits, two inline and the rest in a vector, and Patch()
no longer creates entries. Block ranges are a flat list scanned on
invalidation (also dropping the invalidated blocks' ranges, the old
TODO). Fastmem patch information is a list sorted by code offset (code
is emitted in address order, so inserts are appends) with 24-byte
entries, looked up by binary search when an access faults.

Mario Kart World, 180 s: resident private memory at 150 s 6195 ->
4777 MiB, peak commit 16.0 -> 14.4 GB; frames and frame times unchanged.

(cherry picked from commit 34f81e052c37973a69aeec39e2f996b89c80e4ad)

