=== 278a2a0e05
core: use the 12 GiB layout and auto-stub IPC for Switch 2 titles

Switch 2 titles size their heap from the total memory and stop at the
first unimplemented command, so they only booted with the 12 GiB layout
and EDEN_NX2_AUTO_STUB set by hand. Detect them from main.npdm before the
kernel starts and apply the layout as a per-game value (the configured
layout comes back once the game is closed), and auto-stub unknown
commands from Switch 2 processes.

=== a482274421
build: fixes for clang-cl

- (std::min)/(std::max) where windows.h's macros are visible
- LAMBDA_FORCEINLINE: always_inline under clang (clang-cl defines
  _MSC_VER but rejects [[msvc::forceinline]] on lambdas)
- ImageBase::FindView: find_if, as MSVC's STL takes its vectorized find
  for ImageViewInfo under clang-cl and fails a size assertion
- parentheses for -Wlogical-op-parentheses in the Ampere lowering

=== df081e2f9d
video_core: stop resuming pending channels once the system is powered off

At shutdown is_powered_on is cleared before the GPU thread is stopped.
A channel suspended on a semaphore acquire that can resume then went
through DispatchCalls, which returns at once when powered off, so the
acquire stayed pending and ResumePending counted it as progress forever:
the GPU thread never got back to its stop request and closing the game
hung in ThreadManager::NotifyShutdown.

=== 9fa008fda3
nx2: decode Switch 2 video (nn::codec::video) with FFmpeg

The decoder service used to complete every submitted frame at once
without decoding it. Each decoder service (gsv 902/906/1002 -> TIPC 1)
now decodes on a thread of its own with FFmpeg (VP9, VP8, H.264;
video_core/host1x/nx2_video_decoder), in software or on the host GPU per
the NVDEC emulation setting.

- 101 takes the codec name ("video/vp9") from its A buffer.
- 109 queues the A buffer's frame; the ready event fires while the queue
  has room.
- 311/314 reply a NativeSyncHandle {~syncpoint, 0, value}: the service
  allocates a syncpoint and increments it per finished frame, frames
  filling output images in register/release order like the client's
  ring. 314 waits for the client's release fence before reuse.
- 213 returns the frame's size, pts (TimeSpan, ns) and end of stream.
- gsv 911 creates a decoder image pool (count images in one memory) and
  gsv 961 returns the 128-byte CpuUserImageDescriptor that
  nn::codec::ReadDecoderOutputImage* copy frames out with; images are
  pitch-linear NV12, RGBA or BGRA.
- Each new video command is logged once in full; EDEN_NX2_VDEC_DUMP=<dir>
  saves every 120th decoded frame's luma.

Title 1's Mushroom Cup opening (2560x1440 VP9, 1423 frames) now decodes
in real time and the game goes on to the race. The picture on screen
still stays on the first frame: the game memcpys each frame into GPU
memory and the texture cache keeps its first upload (notes 148).

=== 06087bfc57
nx2: syncpt cancel always signals; a new slot request starts unsignalled

nn::syncpt::sdk's CancelSyncPointEvent sends 202 and then always waits on
the slot's event, so 202 now signals it also when no request was pending.
Title 1's movie player cancels each output-buffer slot before its first
request and hung there forever (the cup-opening screen stayed black and
the decoder was never started).

Slots are reused by different client objects: the movie player got slot
0 after NVN's fence waits had left its event signalled, read that as a
decoded frame, found none and aborted with 2111-3604. 201 now clears the
slot's event before registering the new request.

=== 92f0d80513
nx2: gsv 906 opens a video decoder session

Newer SDKs (nn::codec::video::detail::InitializeServerContext, title 4)
send 906(u8, u64 decoder library version) where older ones send 902.
Auto-stubbed, it returned no handle and title 4 aborted with
InvalidHandle (0xE401) when its first movie started, about 30 minutes
into a new game. 906 now hands out the same session as 902.

=== de004b6e9f
vulkan: leave the alpha test reference out of pipelines that don't test alpha

FixedPipelineState stored alpha_test_ref whatever the alpha test
function. Title 1 animates the register with the test off (text fading
in and out), so every value made a new pipeline whose shaders were
lowered and translated on the GPU thread: bursts of 600-1,900 pipelines
in 10 s (up to 5 s of GPU thread) whenever text was on screen. The
reference is now kept only while the test compares with it (not Always
or Never). Driving title 1's menus: 400+ pipelines for already-known
shaders before, 0 after; one shader lowered 79 times before, 2 after.

EDEN_NX2_PIPELINE_DIFF=1 logs which fixed-state fields differ when a new
pipeline reuses known shaders.

=== ac7ccd35f9
video_core: GPU thread split on by default for Switch 2 titles; cheaper front paths

Clean A/B in title 1 (3 rounds each): 1.46x the draws a second of one
thread, frames 17% shorter, no crashes, front register copies equal to
the back's in 57,089 checks. EDEN_NX2_SPLIT=0 runs everything on one
thread, =1 keeps macros on the back.

The front's per-write paths no longer check guarded statics
(GpuSplit::Get, Nx2Profile::Enabled, the skip-same switches) and the
ring's method writes are inline. Notes row 145.

=== b884c25d6e
nx2: notes 142-144 (GPU thread split, scheduler race, front register copies)

=== 7ef288ce7e
video_core: split the GPU thread in two (EDEN_NX2_SPLIT, off by default)

The GPU thread (front) takes the CPU's commands, parses command lists
and runs the puller; engine method calls and everything behind them
(registers, draws, dispatches, the rasterizer and its caches) run in
order on a second thread (back), fed through a single-producer
single-consumer ring (gpu_split.h). Work the front did on back-owned
state (end of command list flushes, channel binds, semaphore releases,
syncpoint increments, flush/invalidate requests, presentation) is
deferred into the same ring; the front waits for the back only where it
needs a result from that state: semaphore acquires and MME DMA reads.

EDEN_NX2_SPLIT=1 runs macros on the back. EDEN_NX2_SPLIT=2 runs the 3D
and compute macros on the front against copies of the registers they
read (Maxwell3D/KeplerCompute Front*), drops state writes that keep a
register's value there, and sends the rest. EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_VERIFY=1
compares the copies with the back's registers every 4096 macro calls
(0 differences in 40,193 checks in title 1); EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_CHECK=1
logs the stack of any other thread using the Vulkan scheduler while the
back runs. Switch 2 titles, Vulkan, non-accurate GPU only.

Title 1, 3D attract loop: ~44,000 draws a second on one thread, ~47,400
with EDEN_NX2_SPLIT=1, 50,000-66,500 with EDEN_NX2_SPLIT=2.

=== 60308ba40c
vulkan: never flush the scheduler from the async present thread

Swapchain::AcquireNextImage waits for the main scheduler's tick of the
image's last use; when that tick was not submitted yet, Scheduler::Wait
flushed, recording into the current chunk on the present thread while
the GPU thread recorded into it too (corrupt command buffers, crashes
in vkCmdEndRenderPass and in CommandChunk::Record). With async
presentation the present thread now waits up to 20 ms for the
recording thread to submit the tick, and goes on without the wait
otherwise (the copy to the swapchain has its own fences).

=== 9bc673df95
nx2: profile engine method calls and semaphore acquires; notes 140-141

EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE now times engine method calls (the work a split GPU thread's back half
would run) and counts semaphore acquires and those that wait. Title 1: method calls ~343 ms
a second (6.9 million), front-half work (command parsing, macros) ~333 ms, acquires ~2,700 a
second of which ~220 wait. Notes 140 (macro compiler) and 141 (draw setup).

=== 3755efe1c9
video_core: compile Turing MME macro code to x86-64

Title 1 calls macros ~1.3 million times a second; most were replayed from the macro memo,
whose lookups missed the CPU caches inside the GPU thread (~8%), and the rest interpreted.
macro_turing_jit.h compiles all of the uploaded macro memory with Xbyak: each instruction a
block falling through to the next, branches and loops through a jump table, with the
interpreter's semantics (lanes reading operands before either writes, predication as two
variants, the END delay slot, loops, carry). Loop checks only where a loop can end, END
checks only after an END, the instruction limit counted on indirect jumps. Emits and register
reads call back into C++. It is recompiled when the macro code changes and used for 3D and
compute macros on x86-64; EDEN_NX2_MME_JIT=0 goes back to the memo and the interpreter.

Checked against the interpreter on 200,000 recorded calls (registers, data RAM, every
method write, FIFO use): identical. In the game: macro calls 148 ns instead of 174 (two
profiled runs each), no failures, 0 crashes in 3 runs. NX2_GPU_THREAD_SPLIT.md describes
the next step, splitting the GPU thread.

=== a8ee576823
nx2: notes 138-139 (async presentation retested, buffer collector on its own size)

=== 3434220055
buffer_cache: collect on the buffers' own size for Switch 2 titles

The buffer collector compared the whole device's memory use with thresholds from the
device size, so with title 1's textures filling an 8 GB GPU (~6.8 GB used, buffers ~200
MiB of it) it ran aggressively every frame: up to 64 buffers evicted and recreated later,
the GPU-written ones downloaded first with a wait for the GPU (~5% of the GPU thread).
For Switch 2 titles it now runs when the buffers themselves pass 1 GiB (aggressive past
2 GiB); below the aggressive mode it also keeps buffers holding GPU-written data, which
it would have to download. Other titles keep the device-wide thresholds. Buffer
downloads by the collector: 5.4% -> 0%; the cache settles around 540 MiB.

=== 24d0773c53
maxwell_3d: no method dispatch for non-executable register writes; notes 133-137

ProcessMethodCall and the draw manager behind it act only on executable methods, so
CallMethod stops at the dirty flags for the rest (macro replays write ~2.4 million of
those a second). No change beyond run-to-run noise in two profiled runs; it only removes
work. Notes: row 133 corrected (those courses are bound by the GPU thread, not the host
GPU: 20-40% busy), rows 135-137 (same-value writes, native compute registers, 2D blits).

=== 4d8b512cff
kepler_compute: store plain native register writes directly

Most native compute register writes (QMD fields its macros replay for thousands of
dispatches a second) only store the value; they went through ProcessNativeMethod's
macro, QMD, upload and semaphore checks. CallMethod now stores them through a table of
plain methods. ProcessNativeMethod's own time 2.2% -> 1.6% of the GPU thread.

=== 72d9d1b64d
maxwell_3d: drop state register writes that keep the register's value

Title 1 makes ~6 million 3D register writes a second and ~60% of them write the value
the register already holds (counted with EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE), most from macro memo
replays. For registers without side effects (not in execution_mask: draws, clears,
constant buffer data and binds, semaphores, ...) such a write changes nothing, but it
set dirty flags that made the next draw re-check that state. CallMethod and the sunk
writes now skip them after shadow RAM processing. Two profiled runs each: macro calls
169 ns instead of 185, draw setup 5.80 us instead of 5.95. EDEN_NX2_SKIP_SAME=0 turns it
off.

=== 719cd0b315
buffer_cache: one GPU wait for all buffers the collector evicts

The buffer collector downloaded each evicted buffer's GPU-written data with its own
wait for the GPU. Without per-fence downloads (d530bc4) most of title 1's evicted
buffers are GPU-written, and on an 8 GB GPU near its budget it evicts some every frame.
Their ranges now go to one staging buffer, copied with one wait.

=== 5eb7c1944b
nx2: notes 131-134 (buffer downloads, inline upload lookups, VRAM-bound courses, deferred inline invalidation)

=== 3e0b6e8728
vulkan: defer the caches' invalidation for inline writes outside GPU-written data

Title 1 makes thousands of small inline-to-memory writes between draws, and each one
invalidated the buffer, texture, shader and query caches on its own (~7.5% of the GPU
thread after 0fe581f). Guest memory is still written at once, query results pending
there are still dropped at once, and writes into GPU-written buffer data are still
uploaded in place; the buffer, texture and shader caches now get the merged ranges at
the next FlushCaching, which runs before every draw, dispatch, clear, copy and 2D blit.
Inline writes: 4.4% of the GPU thread (deferred invalidation included in FlushCaching,
3.9% -> 3.1%). EDEN_NX2_INLINE_DEFER=0 invalidates at once.

=== 39dd849e24
texture_cache: count evictions that download; EDEN_NX2_TC_MIN_AGE knob

The collector's periodic log now also counts evictions that downloaded the image first
(each a full GPU wait). EDEN_NX2_TC_MIN_AGE=<frames> keeps images used more recently
than that (a VRAM thrash test on 8 GB: 120 frames made no difference over three runs).

=== ead04e6aec
common: pass-through hash for integer and pointer keys; RangeSet::Intersects

Common::unordered_map/set defaulted to std::hash, a byte-wise FNV-1a on MSVC for
integers and pointers, which boost's flat containers then mix again: the GPU thread's
page maps (shader and query cache invalidation, per inline upload) spent ~1% in it.
Integer and pointer keys now go through as they are and boost mixes them.
BufferCache::IsRegionGpuModified visited every GPU-written range inside the query just
to answer yes or no; without per-fence downloads those ranges stay fragmented and it was
~4% of title 1's GPU thread (inline uploads). RangeSet::Intersects answers with one
lookup. Inline uploads: 10.5% -> 7.5% of the GPU thread.

=== 148d0522f3
buffer_cache: no buffer downloads at every fence for Switch 2 titles

Eden copied every GPU-written buffer range back to guest memory at the next fence.
Title 1 signals a fence per compute dispatch, so that was 250-330 MB/s in 13,000-20,000
ranges a second, with the fencing thread holding the buffer cache while it wrote them.
Measured: the native MME's DMA reads never touched those pages (they read query words
written on the CPU side), and with reactive flushing the guest CPU read GPU-written
memory at most 64 times in 200 s. Without them, for Switch 2 titles (native tiling):
fences ~1.1 us instead of ~5, draw setup 6.1 us instead of 6.4-6.7, macro calls 160 ns
instead of 167-170, GPU-thread lock spinning 1.8% (was 5.5%). Regions stay marked GPU
written and are still flushed on demand. EDEN_NX2_BUFFER_DOWNLOADS=0/1 forces either
way. Reactive flushing reads of GPU-written memory are now counted in the log.

=== a6b3589eec
nx2: notes 123-130 (fences, lock handoffs, translation cache crash, macro parameters, counter polling, worker wake-ups, memo compaction, measurement pitfalls)

=== 963e843721
video_core: compact the macro memo arena; download profiling counters

Calls stored again with another shape leave their old record in the memo's arena;
title 1 filled its 16 MiB every ~2 s and clearing it made the next ~20,000 calls
interpret again. The live records are now copied to a new arena (a clear only when
they alone fill three quarters of it). The memo statistics count hits on the last
call's slot and compactions. With EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE the GPU thread also reports
how much downloaded buffer data lands on pages the native MME has DMA-read.

=== 556144e266
vulkan: dispatch to the worker every 32 draws, wake it only when it waits

Every 8 draws the GPU thread handed a command chunk to the Vulkan worker and woke it
with a system call (~2% of the GPU thread at ~55,000 draws a second). Chunks now go
every 32 draws (a full chunk still goes on its own), and the worker is woken only
when it is waiting; a busy worker pops the queue before waiting again.

=== e2054a2d96
core: pause in guest loops that poll the system counter

Title 1's main thread reads CNTPCT ~17 million times a second while it waits for the
GPU (~23% of its samples are the counter read). On an SMT core that spin takes
resources from the sibling thread, often the GPU thread. Two reads within ~1.7 us of
each other now execute two PAUSE instructions first; the guest sees the same values.
EDEN_NX2_CNTPCT_PAUSE=0 turns it off.

=== b0f8b14cce
video_core: copy whole macro calls to the stack

Nearly every macro call arrives whole in one method run (~1,000,000 a second in title
1); its parameters were appended to a std::vector first (~2% of the GPU thread). They
are now copied to a stack array. Still a copy: the command list is often guest memory
read in place, which the guest may reuse while the macro runs, and the macro memo
stores the parameters after the call. Accurate GPU mode keeps the vector (it rewrites
parameters in place). EDEN_NX2_MACRO_COPY=1 always uses the vector.

=== af1b2fd5cd
video_core: release already-signaled fences together

The fencing thread now takes every fence the host GPU has already passed (up to 64)
and pops their texture and buffer downloads under one lock acquisition per cache,
instead of one per fence (title 1 signals 5,000-7,000 a second; the GPU thread spun,
slept and woke the fencing thread for each). Queries and operations keep their
per-fence order. In the sampler, the GPU thread's spinning on the cache locks went
from 7.0% to 5.5%; frame times are within the attract loop's run-to-run noise.
EDEN_NX2_FENCE_BATCH=0 releases one fence at a time.

=== d7d289636d
core: per-thread device memory translation cache

DeviceMemoryManager kept one four-entry page translation cache for every thread. Map
(on a guest service thread, e.g. nx2:gpu 211 job mappings) cleared it while the GPU
thread was between its null check of an entry and the copy from it: the GPU thread
then read from address 0 plus the page offset. Title 1 crashed this way in about half
of its 150 s test runs (BindHostGraphicsUniformBuffer -> ReadBlockUnsafe, fault at
0x600). Each thread now keeps its own cache, which Map and Unmap invalidate through a
generation counter. 0 crashes in 6 runs after.

=== 6cfd9bf041
video_core: skip empty fences; one lock per cache per fence; single-pass overlap subtract

Switch 2 titles signal a fence for each compute dispatch's release (5,000-7,000 a
second in title 1). With fast GPU fences the callback runs at once, so a fence with
no downloads, queries or queued operations does nothing: those (~20%) are no longer
made (one in 64 still is, for the operations fences carry). Each cache's lock is now
taken once per fence (the flush check took both together first). The overlap range
set's Subtract restarted its search after every erase (quadratic); the fencing thread
runs it for every downloaded range while holding the buffer cache. The GPU profiler
counts fences, skipped fences and buffer download bytes.

=== e090f4e946
nx2: notes 120-122 (cache lock contention, shader translation, final measurements)

=== 9472293e0e
nx2: macro memo tries each macro's last call first; FIFO filled only when interpreting

46% of title 1's macro calls repeat the previous call of the same
macro exactly: a per-start slot holding that record's offset skips the
hash and the map probe for them (the record is still in the CPU cache).
The interpreter's parameter FIFO was filled before the memo lookup; it
is now filled only for calls that are interpreted.

Checked against the previous interpreter on 200,000 recorded 3D-scene
calls: identical writes, registers and data RAM.

=== 6967b0f199
shader_recompiler: linear-time breadth-first search; cache storage buffer tracking

Translating title 1's 28,102-instruction compute shader took 3.4 s on
the GPU thread (a freeze the first time it runs), three quarters of it
in GlobalMemoryToStorageBufferPass:
- BreadthFirstSearch checked "visited" with a linear scan per argument,
  quadratic once a search walks thousands of instructions; past 64 it
  now uses a hash set (same visit order, same result).
- The pass searched again for every global memory access; accesses
  through the same address now share the result (the IR does not
  change while storage buffers are collected).
That shader now takes 0.85 s; other new pipelines also cost less.

=== db61b2bf32
vulkan: log runtime pipeline builds on the GPU thread over 2 ms and their total every 10 s

Tells how much of the stutter on new content is shader translation
(the driver compile already runs on workers).

=== fa6b3e1a31
video_core: less waiting on the GPU caches' locks

- The texture and buffer caches' mutexes spin briefly before sleeping
  (Common::SpinRecursiveMutex): the GPU thread, the fencing thread and
  CPU write traps take them tens of thousands of times a second for
  microseconds, and a sleeping waiter's wake-up took longer than that.
  Title 1's GPU thread slept 7.7% of its time there, now 3.7%.
- Fences commit and pop texture and buffer downloads separately, and
  only when there are any (one fence per compute dispatch in Switch 2
  titles): the fencing thread no longer holds both caches while it
  copies buffer downloads.
- Inline uploads and pre-draw invalidation take the caches one at a
  time again: holding one while std::lock waited for the other waited
  longer.

=== ee69e5684c
nx2: notes 117-119 (macro memo, async presentation crashes, tester log findings)

=== daee990471
video_core: cheaper small GPU reads, buffer usage tracking and compute uniforms

- MemoryManager::ReadBlockUnsafe reads within one 4 KiB page through a
  single translation (as Read<T> does) instead of the page walk:
  texture and sampler descriptors, several per draw (2.6% -> 0.1% of
  title 1's GPU thread).
- UsageTracker skips ranges it tracked just before (until Reset): draws
  bind the same multi-megabyte storage buffers again and again, and
  each Track wrote a word per 4 KiB (2.0% -> 0.7%).
- Small compute uniform buffers the GPU has not written are streamed,
  as graphics ones are; synchronizing them re-protected pages an inline
  upload had just unprotected, two VirtualProtect calls per dispatch
  (1.9% -> 0.7%, page protection 3.2% -> 2.3%).

=== 1b852c1e3e
video_core: guard the fence manager's uncommitted operations

SyncOperation appended to uncommitted_operations without a lock while
SignalFence moved that list into pending_operations under `guard` on
another thread; a list corrupted this way crashed the fencing thread.
They now share a lock of their own, held for no callbacks (SignalFence
runs callbacks under `guard`, so taking `guard` here could deadlock).

=== 30b75cd85b
texture_cache: Switch 2 collector thresholds follow the device memory budget

Native titles' thresholds came from Eden's device memory figure, which
is capped near 7 GB: on a tester's RTX 4070 SUPER (11.2 GB budget) title
1 kept evicting 800-1,600 textures per 600 frames with 3 GB unused. They
now follow the budget (critical at budget - 768 MiB, expected at budget
- 1536 MiB, re-read every 60 frames); the EDEN_NX2_TC_* overrides still
win, and the thresholds are logged only when they move by 256 MiB.

=== f5f885d3fc
nx2:vi2: quiet stubs for 104 and 105

Title 1 calls both ~75 times a second with the display name "default".
The auto stub's reply (success, no data) works; it also logged two lines
per call, which made a tester's 18 minute log 50 MB. Same reply, no log.

=== a50d4a80ca
vulkan: one lock for both caches in InnerInvalidation

It runs before most draws (constant buffer updates go through the
invalidation accumulator) and took the texture and buffer cache mutexes
one after the other.

=== a4674d242e
nx2: memo for compute macros; one lock per inline upload; cheaper shader invalidation

- Compute engine macros replay repeated calls like 3D ones (title 1's
  compute macro path fell from ~27% to ~14% of the GPU thread).
- AccelerateInlineToMemory takes the buffer and texture cache mutexes
  together instead of one after the other.
- ShaderCache::InvalidatePagesInRegion looks at the rejected programs
  only when the write can reach one (bounds kept as they are added);
  it ran a locked scan for every inline upload and pre-draw
  invalidation.

Inline uploads went from ~10% to ~4% of the GPU thread.

=== 3434eababe
nx2: macro memo hashes its keys as they are, rewrites re-stored calls in place

The flat map re-hashed the already mixed u64 keys with MSVC's byte-wise
FNV-1a (~1.4% of the GPU thread), and calls stored again after a data
RAM guard failed appended new records, so the arena filled with stale
copies and was cleared every few seconds. EDEN_NX2_MME_MEMO_STATS logs
the counters: in title 1, 97.3% of calls replay, 2.1% fail a guard.

=== ee9e787fc8
nx2: poll pending channels briefly before sleeping; profile zones on the TSC

- A channel waiting on a semaphore the CPU releases slept 1 ms per poll
  (~25 waits a second in title 1); poll for 0.5 ms first.
- The GPU profile zones read the wall clock (the TSC) instead of
  QueryPerformanceCounter, which cost ~5% of the GPU thread when on.

=== 8d83cef520
nx2: replay repeated native macro calls instead of interpreting them

Title 1 makes ~1,000,000 native macro calls a second in 3D scenes, 7
instructions each on average, and 96% repeat an earlier call exactly.
A call whose effects depend only on its start, its parameters and the
data RAM words it read (no MME_READ, no memory reads, no re-read of
its own data RAM writes, no code upload while it ran) is remembered
with those reads as guards; a later call with the same start and
parameters whose guards still hold replays its data RAM and method
writes. Uploading macro code clears the memo. EDEN_NX2_MME_MEMO=0
interprets every call.

Checked offline against the previous interpreter on 200,000 recorded
3D-scene calls (method writes, registers and data RAM all identical,
94% replayed, 1.9x faster); in the game the macro zone fell from ~0.26
to ~0.20 us per call.

=== ab80749e94
nx2: present stats report the worst frame and frames over 100 ms; notes 110-116

The per-600-present log only had the average frame time, which hides
shader-compile hitches; it now also gives the longest frame and how
many took over 100 ms.

Notes: today's profile of title 1 and what came of it (TSC on AMD,
rejected-shader rethrows, debug hooks, texture churn from VMA slack,
reactive flushing, macro interpreter).

=== d5d4b61b5b
nx2: EDEN_NX2_MME_RECORD records macro calls for offline interpreter checks

Writes the first 200,000 native macro calls (start, parameters) and the
macro code, so an interpreter change can be replayed against the
previous interpreter (all 200,000 matched for the operand-slot rewrite,
which was then dropped as no faster).

=== ed9262e642
vulkan: 64 MiB VMA blocks; device memory usage without VMA block slack

Title 1's texture cache deleted 1,400-2,600 textures per 600 frames
and created them again: device memory sat at the collector's critical
line, but ~38% of it was empty space in 256 MiB VMA blocks. Freeing an
image returns that space only once its whole block is empty, so the
caches kept collecting without the usage dropping (one mass purge
deleted 4,300 textures).

- 64 MiB blocks on discrete GPUs too (as on integrated ones).
- GetDeviceMemoryUsage subtracts the unused bytes in VMA blocks, so
  each eviction counts at once.

Now 0-224 deletions per 600 frames, ~4,400 textures resident, and
driver usage 6.24 GB of a 6.97 GB budget.

=== a89892695e
kernel: spin briefly before sleeping on a contended KSpinLock

The scheduler lock is taken by every emulated core thousands of times a
frame (title 1's job system: WaitForAddress / SignalToAddress / yield)
and held for microseconds. As a std::mutex, a contended Lock put the
host thread to sleep at once. Now a three-state futex lock: one atomic
op uncontended, ~128 pause spins when contended, then
std::atomic::wait. Time blocked in Lock fell ~20-25% on title 1's cores.

=== 2671edfe36
nx2: measure the TSC on AMD, no rethrows for rejected shaders, debug hooks off the hot path

- AMD CPUs have no CPUID leaf 0x15, so the TSC frequency stayed 0 and
  every guest CNTPCT read went through QueryPerformanceCounter (~10% of
  the game's main core in title 1). Measure it like Intel CPUs without
  a crystal frequency.
- A rejected native shader was rethrown (two C++ throws) every time it
  was bound; return false instead (~1-2% of the GPU thread).
- The semaphore watch, pushbuffer entry hashing, first-write trace and
  per-macro debug logs ran on every command word or macro call; they
  now cost a flag check unless their environment variable is set
  (~4% of the GPU thread). Also starts the macro engine's profile zone.

=== 51791d0fa4
nx2: GPU-thread zone timers and an in-process sampling profiler

EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE=1 times the GPU thread's stages (command parsing,
macros, draw setup by step, compute, texture uploads) and logs each
one's share of every second.

EDEN_NX2_SAMPLE=<thread names> (Windows) pauses the named threads about
600 times a second for EDEN_NX2_SAMPLE_SECONDS, walks their stacks and
writes per-function counts plus folded stacks (EDEN_NX2_SAMPLE_OUT).

=== 8c0579e555
nx2:gpu: free GPU-only region memory when the region is destroyed

Sub 215 dropped the region's GPU mapping but kept the kernel pages 214
had allocated for it, and their SMMU mapping (cached per CPU range).
Title 1 creates and destroys these regions as scenes load, so the pool
ran dry within minutes and later regions got no memory at all (37
allocation failures in 85 s, 165 in a longer run): GPU writes to them
were lost.

Destroying a region now drops its GPU mapping, then (through a new
registry release step, in GPU-thread order) each address space's SMMU
mapping and cache entry, and frees the pages once the GPU thread is
past both. A 4-minute run destroyed 116 regions with no later failures
and renders cleanly; only the 5.8 GB virtual texture regions and three
170-340 MB regions at startup still do not fit the pool.

=== dcf6212ddd
nx2: video decoder service protocol (no decoding yet)

Read from the SDK's nn::codec VP9 decoder: the decoder service session
(vdec TIPC 1) takes 200 initialize (work buffer transfer memory), 101
configure (X: codec configuration, width, height), 3 its submission
event (copy handle at word 3), 6/207/8 start/stop/flush, 115 finalize,
311/314/212 register/release/unregister an output image (reply a
16-byte NativeSyncHandle; all zero reads as signalled), 109 submit a
frame (A: bitstream, u64 pts, u8 end of stream) and 213 the next
finished frame (image id, size, colour space, pts, end of stream).
Output images come from gsv 911, the same request as 429.

The service completes each submitted frame at once into the next free
image (no picture yet) and keeps its event signalled. Title 1 now sets
the decoder up without aborting; in the runs so far it has not submitted
frames yet. Auto-stubbed requests on nx2:vdec* sessions log 48 words.

=== 8965d72141
nx2: present no longer waits for its own frame; cancelled slot events signal

- vi2 229 now waits for the previous frame's fence by default, so the
  game prepares frame N+1 while the GPU thread runs frame N. Waiting for
  the current frame serialized them: 8-56 ms of each 34-105 ms frame was
  spent blocked. Title 1 now blocks under 1 ms per frame on average and
  its GPU thread runs ~90% busy, which is the next limit.
  EDEN_NX2_FRAMES_AHEAD=0 restores the old wait.
- syncpt 202 (cancel a slot request) signals the slot's event when the
  request was still pending. NvOsEventPool waits on it again after its
  ~100 ms wait times out; title 1 hung there forever whenever a mid-frame
  wait outlasted 100 ms with the GPU thread a frame behind (2 of 4
  runs). Two 185 s runs with 2-3 cancels each completed.
- EDEN_NX2_VSYNC_HZ=<rate> sets the vi2 vsync event rate (experiment;
  240 Hz did not shorten title 1's frames).

