Keep huge ASTC arrays from a lower mip on the host (title 1 terrain)

Title 1's terrain virtual textures are 8192x8192x68 ASTC 8x8 sRGB arrays with
12 levels: 1.5 GB in guest memory (fully backed) but 23 GB each as the RGBA8
Eden decodes ASTC to, so they were created unbacked and the near terrain
sampled black. A GPU-decoded ASTC 2D array over 1 GiB decoded now keeps only
the levels from the first one that fits in 512 MiB (here 3: 1024x1024, 380
MiB); host mip 0 is that guest level. Uploads read each layer's tail of the
mip chain into a compact buffer and decode only those levels, views shift
their level range, and these images are never downloaded. Normalized
coordinates and derivative LOD address the smaller image correctly; the
nearest terrain is lower resolution than on the console.

Also logs how unbacked images are used (views, render targets, blits, DMA).

=== 6d7acfb990
Fix the P6 scratch save in R2UR/MATCH/HFMA2.RELU; GPU memory dump tool

The colour cast on title 1 (magenta/cyan, then red/green, changing per
course) came from the sky/atmosphere volume shader (a pixel shader drawing
a 32^3 RGBA16F table slice by slice), which starts with an unguarded R2UR.
R2UR, MATCH and HFMA2.RELU use P6 as scratch and saved/restored it with the
uniform-guard mask, 1 << (6 - 7) for an unguarded instruction: undefined, in
practice bit 31, which shifted into the opcode bits and turned the save into
another instruction. They now always save and restore P6.

Debugging aids used to find it:
- EDEN_NX2_DUMP=<present>:<addr>:<size>,...: flush GPU memory ranges at a
  present (on the GPU thread) and save them next to the frame captures;
  nx2work/vol3d.py untiles Switch 2 3D textures from such dumps.
- GPU::RunOnGpuThread.
- Logs of Switch 2 3D images and descriptors.

