=== fcd02f8778
nx2: unmap GPU memory in submission order

NormalUnmapGpuMemory4K and destroying GPU memory are jobs the console's
graphics server completes behind the work already submitted. Run the GMMU
unmaps on the GPU thread after the command lists queued before them
(GPU::RunInOrder) and signal the job's event only then, instead of unmapping
and signalling at once while Eden's GPU thread may still be behind.

Also EDEN_NX2_FRAMES_AHEAD (experimental): wait for the previous frame's
fence at present instead of this one's.

=== 36b6b30452
nx2: ATOMG and local spills with an immediate offset

ATOMG.E.ADD through a uniform pointer and STL/LDL.32 at RZ only took a zero
offset; title 4's loading screen uses [UR + 4] and [RZ + 4]. Maxwell ATOM
has a signed 20-bit offset at bits 28-47, STL/LDL a 24-bit one at 20-43.

=== a89b3087a9
nx2: method data continues across GPFIFO entries

Title 4 calls a 3D macro with a 6-word IncreaseOnce header, ends the main
GPFIFO entry after the first word and supplies the other five in a 5-word
subroutine entry. The split-method parking (an entry elsewhere was treated
as a call with a clean method state) fed those parameters to the parser as
headers: the macro ran with garbage, later draws read megabytes of unmapped
memory and the host driver timed out at the loading screen after the dock
scene. As on the hardware, the method state now simply continues; the old
guess stays behind EDEN_NX2_PARK_SPLIT_METHODS.

Debug aids: splits are logged at debug level with the words around them,
and a 3D write that interrupts a macro's parameters dumps the last 48 writes.

=== 5046a37c95
nx2: notes for the semaphore, pacing and open-file fixes

=== c81a146fd5
vfs: keep the open-file cache below the CRT limit

The real VFS kept up to 8192 host files open, which is also the most the
Windows C runtime allows (_setmaxstdio(8192)). A game read from a loose RomFS
folder fills that within minutes (title 4: ~7300 handles after 160 s); from
then on every other open failed with "too many files open" - its next scene
pack, its save file, the shader cache - and the game waited forever on the
load. Cap the cache 512 below _getmaxstdio() on Windows.

=== 42989ad8a9
nx2: pace Switch 2 presents to a 60 Hz vblank

The console flips one frame per vblank and holds the rest in the swapchain,
which caps a game at 60 fps. vi2 229 reports every buffer slot free at once,
so nothing paced title 4: it presented at 90-160 fps and its frame-stepped
timing, audio included, ran ahead. Hold each present until the next vblank,
scaled by the speed limit, without banking time lost to slow frames.

=== 5d27ccd0a0
nx2: semaphore releases latch the acquires they satisfy

Switch 2 channels that wait for an exact semaphore value poll on the console
and see every release. Eden resumes a waiting channel only between command
lists and writes releases when fences signal, often several back to back, so
an acquire-equal could miss its value and wait forever. Title 4's 3D queue
ticks a counter that two other queues wait on value by value; under the
loading after the post-customization save one of them missed a tick and the
game froze (channel 4 waiting for 0x5293 with the counter at 0x5310).

Pending acquires now register with Nx2SemaphoreWaiters before fences run,
and every query-cache payload write and QMD add/max release latches the
waiters it meets at the moment it is written.

=== fd768d8530
nx2: notes for the freed-memory crash and the ASTC UI fix

=== 034b9cab96
nx2: decode ASTC textures with the Switch 2 GOB layout

The GPU ASTC decoder unswizzles block-linear data itself and never applied
the native (Switch 2) GOB swizzle that the other unswizzle paths use, so
ASTC textures came out with 16-byte blocks swapped wherever the image
varies. Title 4's UI is ASTC (10x5, 4x4, 8x8): portraits, buttons and the
squid cursor had hatched edges and clean interiors. Pass the native tiling
flag to the decoder and swap offset bits 5 and 6 as
block_linear_unswizzle_2d.comp does.

=== 910e56d372
nx2: unmap GPU memory the game frees

nx2:gpu 213 (NormalUnmapGpuMemory4K) and 102 (destroy GPU-accessible
memory) were no-ops, so Eden kept freed memory mapped for the GPU. Title 4
frees its customization-room memory right after saving and reuses it as
ordinary heap; pending GPU->CPU write-backs for the old buffers then landed on
the new objects, and two threads crashed on garbage pointers ~5 s later
(the post-customization freeze).

Unmap the range from every address space (the GMMU unmap drops cached
buffers/images and their pending downloads), keep the registry's mapping
list in step, re-map 211 requests even at the memory's own address, and
drop freed regions from the semaphore mirror table, which every 8 KiB
allocation used to fill for good after 32 entries.

